1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,374
Lesson 3.3: Formulating the Care Plan's Problem Note.

2
00:00:05,414 --> 00:00:08,402
Part 2: Medication Adherence.

3
00:00:10,067 --> 00:00:14,749
Medication adherence means the patient takes the medications as prescribed.

4
00:00:14,789 --> 00:00:18,836
You check it in the medication administration record.

5
00:00:18,886 --> 00:00:22,463
Check adherence only for the CCM medications: here,

6
00:00:22,503 --> 00:00:26,780
rosuvastatin. Not the patient's other medications.

7
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:31,718
For a scratch patient, check exactly 30 days, counting back from today.

8
00:00:31,768 --> 00:00:35,327
If today is November 13, that's from October 13:

9
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:38,090
so you read two monthly reports.

10
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:41,305
Every scheduled dose in those 30 days must be given,

11
00:00:41,345 --> 00:00:44,008
or have a reason you can document.

12
00:00:45,433 --> 00:00:48,150
You check adherence from the same Orders tab.

13
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:52,916
In the top right corner, open Reports.

14
00:00:53,854 --> 00:00:57,604
It lists several reports. Choose Administration Record.

15
00:01:01,567 --> 00:01:06,254
The report setup opens. Keep the report type: Monthly Reports.

16
00:01:07,633 --> 00:01:12,947
Under Administration Records, check Medication Administration Record, or MAR.

17
00:01:14,067 --> 00:01:15,490
Then choose the month.

18
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:19,401
For a scratch patient, you check adherence for exactly 30 days,

19
00:01:19,441 --> 00:01:21,451
counting back from today.

20
00:01:21,501 --> 00:01:27,902
If today is November 13, 2026, you check from October 13 to today.

21
00:01:29,567 --> 00:01:31,726
So you need two reports: November,

22
00:01:31,766 --> 00:01:36,998
for November 1 to today, and October, for October 13 to 31.

23
00:01:38,877 --> 00:01:41,966
Start with November. Then click Run Report.

24
00:01:43,667 --> 00:01:45,972
The report opens as a PDF.

25
00:01:46,033 --> 00:01:50,128
Each medication has a row, with a box for every day of the month.

26
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:55,673
Take alendronate sodium. On Sunday, the 1st, it's marked X.

27
00:01:56,933 --> 00:02:02,442
Its directions: one tablet, once a day, every Wednesday, for osteoporosis.

28
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,016
So X marks the days it isn't scheduled. The patient isn't meant to take it then.

29
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:12,520
On Wednesdays, the box is checked,

30
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:15,804
with the nurse's initials: the dose was given.

31
00:02:17,733 --> 00:02:23,606
You only check adherence for the CCM medications. Here, that's rosuvastatin.

32
00:02:25,267 --> 00:02:27,019
Search the report for it.

33
00:02:28,133 --> 00:02:34,157
Rosuvastatin Calcium Tablet 20 MG: one tablet at bedtime, every day.

34
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:41,203
Every day, from the 1st to yesterday, the 12th, is checked.

35
00:02:41,267 --> 00:02:45,222
The patient took it on every date. So the patient is adherent.

36
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:52,699
When a day isn't checked, find out why the dose wasn't given.

37
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:57,745
The Chart Codes table, at the end of the report, explains each code.

38
00:02:59,933 --> 00:03:03,428
R means refused: the patient refused that dose.

39
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:09,074
HD means the dose was held. See the nurse notes.

40
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:14,483
NG means the parameters were out of range, so the medication wasn't given.

41
00:03:15,667 --> 00:03:16,499
For example,

42
00:03:16,539 --> 00:03:22,824
a blood pressure medication like losartan is held when the patient's blood pressure is too low.

43
00:03:25,567 --> 00:03:30,264
HO means hospitalized, and AW means away from the center.

44
00:03:31,567 --> 00:03:36,751
This patient took rosuvastatin on every date, so there are no adherence issues.

45
00:03:39,133 --> 00:03:44,091
Under Medication Adherence, write: patient is adherent to medications.

46
00:03:45,767 --> 00:03:49,290
If you find refusals, the patient is non-adherent.

47
00:03:53,633 --> 00:03:57,230
Write which medication was refused, and on which dates.

48
00:03:58,367 --> 00:04:01,741
If a dose was held because the parameters were out of range,

49
00:04:01,781 --> 00:04:03,778
the patient is still adherent.

50
00:04:09,167 --> 00:04:12,024
Write that the medication wasn't taken on those dates,

51
00:04:12,064 --> 00:04:14,907
because the parameters were out of range.

52
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:21,957
A scheduled day with no check mark and no code is a blank day.

53
00:04:22,007 --> 00:04:25,706
Here, Wednesday the 18th is blank because it hasn't happened yet.

54
00:04:25,746 --> 00:04:29,685
Inside your 30 days, a blank day needs checking.

55
00:04:29,735 --> 00:04:32,832
A blank day is a documentation error. But first,

56
00:04:32,872 --> 00:04:36,577
check whether the patient was in the facility that day.

57
00:04:36,633 --> 00:04:40,757
The patient may have been in the hospital, or on a leave of absence.

58
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:44,786
For the rest of your 30 days,

59
00:04:44,826 --> 00:04:48,346
run the report again, with the month set to October.

60
00:04:51,141 --> 00:04:52,678
Click Run Report.

61
00:04:55,033 --> 00:04:59,435
Search for rosuvastatin, and check October 13 to 31.

62
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:07,597
Every day is checked. There are no adherence issues, so the patient is adherent.

63
00:05:09,067 --> 00:05:11,645
Here is every scenario you can find in the record,

64
00:05:11,685 --> 00:05:14,098
and what you document for each.

65
00:05:14,148 --> 00:05:19,277
The patient took the medication on every relevant date: the patient is adherent.

66
00:05:19,327 --> 00:05:22,547
Refusals, marked R: the patient is non-adherent.

67
00:05:22,587 --> 00:05:27,155
Document which medication was refused, and on which dates.

68
00:05:27,205 --> 00:05:30,910
Held per parameters, marked NG: the patient is adherent,

69
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:35,629
despite missing those doses, because the parameters were out of range.

70
00:05:35,679 --> 00:05:39,898
Blank days while the patient was hospitalized, or out of the facility:

71
00:05:39,938 --> 00:05:44,424
adherent. The doses were missed because the patient was away.

72
00:05:44,474 --> 00:05:47,146
Blank days while the patient was in the facility:

73
00:05:47,186 --> 00:05:51,867
still adherent, but mark the blank days as a documentation error.

74
00:05:51,917 --> 00:05:53,505
Marked X: find out why.

75
00:05:53,545 --> 00:05:58,680
Check whether the medication was prescribed on those dates, or what else happened.

76
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,499
To check whether the patient was out of the facility, use the progress notes.

77
00:06:08,438 --> 00:06:11,565
Everything that happens to the patient is documented there.

78
00:06:11,605 --> 00:06:13,884
Open the Prog Notes tab.

79
00:06:18,733 --> 00:06:22,045
Make sure Display eMAR Progress Notes is checked,

80
00:06:22,085 --> 00:06:25,761
so notes about medication administration show.

81
00:06:27,141 --> 00:06:31,197
Click View All. Stay on Progress Notes, not Practitioner Notes.

82
00:06:35,911 --> 00:06:39,079
You can filter the notes by date. Click Custom.

83
00:06:43,933 --> 00:06:49,649
Set the effective date range to your 30 days: October 13 to today. Then save.

84
00:06:53,067 --> 00:06:55,763
Now search the notes for hospitalization.

85
00:06:57,967 --> 00:07:01,422
This fall risk evaluation from October 21 says:

86
00:07:01,462 --> 00:07:06,256
recent hospitalization history in the last 30 days, no.

87
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:10,186
Search for refusals too. Here, there are none.

88
00:07:12,701 --> 00:07:15,209
To confirm, open the Census tab.

89
00:07:16,867 --> 00:07:20,245
It lists the patient's status over time. Since July 1,

90
00:07:20,285 --> 00:07:23,510
the patient has been active in the facility.

91
00:07:26,233 --> 00:07:29,059
A hospital stay shows up as its own row.

92
00:07:29,109 --> 00:07:35,742
Here, on June 10, 2026, the patient went to the hospital for less than 24 hours,

93
00:07:35,782 --> 00:07:37,837
and was back the same day.

94
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:42,127
A leave of absence, like this therapeutic leave, shows up here too.

95
00:07:42,177 --> 00:07:45,131
That's everything you check for medication adherence.

96
00:07:50,267 --> 00:07:53,338
To recap: check only the CCM medications,

97
00:07:53,378 --> 00:07:58,154
for exactly 30 days, in the November and October reports.

98
00:07:58,204 --> 00:08:01,446
Given on every date: the patient is adherent.

99
00:08:01,496 --> 00:08:05,596
Refused: non-adherent. Name the medication and the dates.

100
00:08:05,646 --> 00:08:09,214
Held because the parameters were out of range: adherent.

101
00:08:09,264 --> 00:08:12,974
Blank while the patient was out of the facility: adherent.

102
00:08:13,024 --> 00:08:15,998
Blank while the patient was in the facility: adherent,

103
00:08:16,038 --> 00:08:19,823
with the blank days marked as a documentation error.

104
00:08:19,873 --> 00:08:23,703
Marked X: find out why, starting with the order's dates.

105
00:08:23,753 --> 00:08:25,076
And use the progress notes

106
00:08:25,116 --> 00:08:29,152
and the census to confirm hospital stays and refusals.

107
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:33,936
Next, you document lab and vital trends,

108
00:08:33,976 --> 00:08:37,215
gaps in care, risk factors and symptoms.
